\f1\fs22 She woke like someone surfacing from deep water, gasping. Her heart felt wrong, arrhythmic. Breathing. She focused on breathing, even and deep. The room resolved around her in moonlight\'97four times the size of her flat, furniture sketched in shadow. Portraits of dimly featured dead white females looked down, grey and serene, from walls glowing with moonlight or some low-grade phosphorescence. Kathy had migrated to a couch by the window; perhaps Sarah had tossed too much in the darker hours.\
Sarah was thirsty. She stood carefully\'97her head still felt untethered, floating above her neck\'97and padded to the ensuite. She turned on the tap and watched water sparkle into the basin. She looked up at her reflection.\
\'93Alexander. How can I marry Alexander?\'94\
Her reflection stared back, offering no answers. If she didn\'92t accept Eloise\'92s Faustian bargain, what would become of her? How could she say no to a woman who could shut down military hardware with a phone call?\
\'93You can\'92t marry him. You can\'92t marry anyone.\'94\
She watched a tear track down her cheek as if it belonged to someone else. Her reflection sobbed. Sarah splashed water over her face, filled a glass and drained it with thirsty gulps, then took a refill to a chair opposite the sleeping Kathy.\
She couldn\'92t marry Alexander. The man was a grotesque caricature of everything wrong with wealth and power, and she\'92d be bound to him and his mother for life. The thought made her want to shower, possibly till the end of time.\
But.\
As reprehensible as Eloise\'92s proposition was, the upsides titillated the deeply entrained appetites of her Airborne lineage. Every Airborne was raised on the dream of ascent\'97higher, always higher, farther above the Layer. The higher one climbed, the more virtuous one was considered. The most insufferable Airborne believed they were prototypical angels, and expected halos would soon form above their truly ascendant heads. Even while Sarah knew that to be wombat shit, she couldn\'92t entirely escape the logic. The climb was everything. The means were secondary to the ascent. And Eloise Shakti was offering her altitude she\'92d never dreamed possible.\
In her entire life, nothing cast Sarah as low as becoming an incubator with legs. As remote as starting a family always was for her, she\'92d expected some kind of joy. Instead, it sat in her stomach like a stone, cold and indigestible. Just for once, for a generation or three, men should get taxing pregnant. They should carry babies\'97those ungrateful passengers. Men should feel that mad-animal fear of carrying something in their gut that was half stranger.\
She sipped water and tried to remember when she\'92d last felt genuinely happy.\
Offending her friends, probably. Dragging them through Cloudborne neighbourhoods and Elvi bars, watching their faces as they tried to maintain composure while surrounded by people they\'92d been taught were the underclass of the underclass. She loved the circus of it. The danger\'97real or imagined\'97of being surrounded by clones who lived fast and died young. She\'92d treated Elvi bars like exotic petting zoos, with slightly more violence and significantly better music. When fights broke out\'97and they always did, with fists and pool cues and nunchucks and bathroom sinks\'97she felt more alive than at any Airborne party, with their elegant restraint and careful hierarchies.\
Sarah always knew the official line about the Elvi was bullshit. They were people, just birthed differently. Their short lifespans were a function of poverty and genetic roulette in a casino that only ever paid the house and its owners.\
She had always known this. The knowing was never the hard part.\
Sarah grew drowsy. She slumped in her chair and smiled at sleeping Kathy. Kathy had known Tony for\'97how long? Years, probably. And Sarah had only known him since mid-February, the first day of her new job. Now it was\'97Tax it. Baby brain already? Her tablet was by the bed, too far to fetch to check the date. May, it was May, still months before the expected Christmas birth.\
Mr Fairy Boxers. The thought of him brought an unwilling smile to her lips. Quickly the nagging question ambushed her, as it often did when she thought of Tony. Is the baby his? Since the news of her pregnancy, Tony was singularly fawning, attentive in ways almost performative, as if he were playing the role of expectant father and hoping no one noticed he was reading from a script.\

\f2\i Cow
\f1\i0 , Sarah muttered at herself. Tony had been nothing but kind, and here she was, imagining ulterior motives.\
But.\
Kathy\'92s evidence suggested Little Clinton carried the DNA of a long-dead scientist, which should have settled the question. Sarah remained sceptical of the science, or perhaps of her own memory. What were the odds that a drunk man would think it was fine to touch a passed-out woman? High. Approaching certainty, really.\
Why couldn\'92t she find someone among her Airborne with all the qualities she liked about Tony? They can\'92t all be dicks, can they? Perhaps belief in halos and angels made it so.\
From the couch, Kathy murmured something in her sleep. It might have been a name. Sarah strained to hear, but it dissolved into the hum of the building\'92s climate system, leaving only the faintest residue of unease.
